---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import ProductCard from "../components/ProductCard.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Deck Stains",
    "url": "/best-deck-stain-reviews/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Deck Stain for Cedar",
    "url": "/best-deck-stain-for-cedar/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Deck Sealant",
    "url": "/best-deck-sealant/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How Long Deck Stain Takes to Dry",
    "url": "/deck-stain-drying-time/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Deck Stain Colors Guide",
    "url": "/deck-stain-colors-guide/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-02", modified: "2026-11-02" });
const title = "Pressure-Treated Deck Stain: 6 Picks That Soak Into PT Pine";
const description =
  "The six stains that suit pressure-treated pine best, how to tell when new PT boards are dry enough, and which stain types to avoid on treated decking.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "ready-seal-natural-cedar",
    "name": "Ready Seal Natural Cedar Exterior Stain and Sealer",
    "category": "Oil-based penetrating stain",
    "badge": "Best Overall for PT Pine",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Penetrating oil that suits porous treated pine",
      "Label says no back-brushing or primer required",
      "Built to avoid lap marks on long deck boards",
      "Natural Cedar tone hides the green cast of new PT",
      "Recoats without stripping"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Shows a bright orange hue for the first several days",
      "Lighter pigment means more frequent floor recoats in hot sun",
      "Solvent odor during application"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The easiest way to get an even, good-looking finish on a treated deck without prior staining experience.",
    "description": "Treated pine soaks up stain fast and unevenly, which is exactly where Ready Seal earns its spot. The oil-based formula is designed to be sprayed, rolled or brushed without back-brushing, and the manufacturer says it will not leave lap marks if you stop mid-board, which matters on a deck where boards run 16 feet. Natural Cedar is the shade most people choose for treated lumber because its warm orange-brown neutralizes the slight green tint left by copper-based preservatives. Expect the color to look brighter than the sample for the first few days; it settles to its final tone over roughly two weeks as the solvent leaves the wood. The pigment load is moderate, so floors on sunny decks often need a refresh after one to two years, but that refresh is just a cleaning and a fresh coat, with no stripping. For a first treated deck, it is the lowest-risk choice we know of. It also sells in 5-gallon pails, which keeps a large deck to one consistent color batch and usually lowers the cost per gallon."
  },
  {
    "id": "twp-1500",
    "name": "TWP 1500 Series Wood Protector (Cedartone)",
    "category": "Oil-based semi-transparent stain",
    "badge": "Best for Mildew-Prone Decks",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "VOC-compliant formula sold in all 50 states",
      "Contains mildewcides for damp or shaded decks",
      "Deep penetration on rough and smooth PT lumber",
      "Long track record with deck restoration pros"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Two wet-on-wet coats often needed on dry, porous wood",
      "Strong solvent smell",
      "Over-application can leave a tacky surface"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right choice for treated decks under trees or in humid climates where mildew keeps coming back.",
    "description": "The TWP 1500 Series is the VOC-compliant counterpart to the long-running TWP 100 Series, which means you can buy it anywhere in the country. It is an oil-based semi-transparent stain with mildewcides and fungicides in the formula, which makes it particularly useful on treated decks that stay damp, sit under tree canopy or face north. Pressure-treated pine is usually porous enough that TWP recommends a second coat applied wet-on-wet, meaning the second coat goes on before the first has dried, so both soak in together. Cedartone is the classic color on treated lumber because it covers the green tint well and holds up to sun better than lighter shades. Like any penetrating oil, it should be applied thin with any excess wiped up, because a puddled coat stays sticky. Owners commonly report a two-year maintenance cycle on the floor and longer on railings. If you live in a state where the original 100 Series is legal, the two are close enough that you can pick on price and availability."
  },
  {
    "id": "armstrong-clark-semi-trans",
    "name": "Armstrong Clark Semi-Transparent Wood Stain",
    "category": "Oil-based penetrating stain",
    "badge": "Best Long-Term Value",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Blend of drying and non-drying oils that stays flexible",
      "Wears by fading rather than peeling",
      "Semi-solid option for older, blotchy treated boards",
      "Simple maintenance coats with no stripping"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Not stocked in most big-box stores",
      "Higher price per gallon than store brands",
      "Needs mineral spirits for cleanup"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Worth ordering if you want a treated deck that stays easy to maintain for the next decade.",
    "description": "Armstrong Clark combines oils that cure in the wood with oils that stay supple, and the goal is a finish that moves with treated pine as it swells and shrinks through the seasons. Treated lumber checks and cracks more than many species as it dries, and a flexible penetrating finish is less likely to split along those cracks than a film-former. The semi-transparent line shows the grain and gives meaningful UV protection; the semi-solid version is a good step up for older treated decks with patched boards or uneven graying. The biggest advantage shows up at year two or three: the stain has faded evenly instead of flaking, so the maintenance coat is a cleaning, a brightener and one thin coat. It costs more than a store-brand gallon and usually has to be ordered online or from a specialty dealer, but on a treated deck that you plan to keep for years, the lower maintenance effort pays it back. It is also one of the few premium stains that many deck contractors will recommend for do-it-yourself owners without hedging."
  },
  {
    "id": "defy-extreme",
    "name": "DEFY Extreme Wood Stain (Semi-Transparent)",
    "category": "Water-based penetrating stain",
    "badge": "Best Water-Based",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Zinc oxide nano-particles for UV resistance",
      "Low odor and water cleanup",
      "Low VOC and legal in every state",
      "Fast dry lets you finish in one day"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Quick drying can cause lap marks in heat",
      "Needs thorough cleaning and brightening for adhesion",
      "Traffic paths may need touch-ups sooner"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The strongest water-based option for treated pine if you want low odor and fast turnaround.",
    "description": "For homeowners who would rather avoid solvent-based stain, DEFY Extreme is the water-based product that performs most like a penetrating oil on treated pine. Its UV protection comes from zinc oxide nano-particles, which help the pigment hold color in direct sun, and it is formulated to soak into the surface rather than build a thick film. That matters on treated lumber, which keeps moving as it dries and will crack a film-forming coating. DEFY dries quickly, so you can usually clean, stain and walk on the deck within a long weekend, and the low-VOC formula makes it legal everywhere. Because it dries fast, work in the shade, keep a wet edge and do two or three boards at a time from end to end. Clean and brighten the treated wood thoroughly beforehand; water-based stains are less tolerant of dirty or mill-glazed surfaces than oils are. Owners who skip the brightener are the ones who most often report early wear on stair treads and traffic paths."
  },
  {
    "id": "cabot-australian-timber-oil",
    "name": "Cabot Australian Timber Oil",
    "category": "Translucent oil finish",
    "badge": "Best Rich Wood Tone",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Linseed and tung oil blend penetrates deeply",
      "Translucent finish gives treated pine a richer look",
      "Easy to find at paint and home stores",
      "Wide range of warm wood-tone colors"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Translucent pigment means yearly touch-ups in full sun",
      "Slow to dry in cool or humid weather",
      "Heavy coats turn tacky"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Pick it when you want treated pine to look like a more expensive wood and you do not mind more frequent recoats.",
    "description": "Cabot Australian Timber Oil is marketed largely for hardwoods, but it also gives pressure-treated pine a surprisingly upscale look. Its oil blend, which includes linseed and tung oil, penetrates the open grain of treated pine readily, and the translucent tints such as Honey Teak and Natural warm up the wood while letting the grain pattern show. The result looks more like a furniture finish than a typical cedar-toned deck. That beauty comes with a maintenance cost. Translucent finishes carry less pigment than semi-transparent stains, so they give up color to UV more quickly; plan on a floor refresh roughly every year in full sun. Apply one thin, even coat, wipe up any puddles promptly and avoid application on cool, damp days, when the oil can take a long time to cure. On a shaded or covered treated deck, it is one of the best-looking options available. Store any leftover with the lid tight, because a partly used can of oil can skin over in a season."
  },
  {
    "id": "behr-premium-semi-transparent",
    "name": "Behr Premium Semi-Transparent Waterproofing Stain & Sealer",
    "category": "Water-based semi-transparent stain",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Low cost per gallon",
      "Tinted in store in dozens of colors",
      "Water cleanup and low odor",
      "Easy to buy in quantity at Home Depot"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Tends to wear faster on horizontal surfaces than premium oils",
      "Less forgiving of lap marks in heat",
      "Needs careful prep to bond well"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A sensible budget stain for a treated deck if you are willing to recoat on a shorter cycle.",
    "description": "Behr Premium Semi-Transparent Waterproofing Stain and Sealer is the budget-friendly choice for treated decks, and its biggest advantage is accessibility. It is tinted at the store in a long list of colors, it is sold in 1-gallon and 5-gallon sizes, and it cleans up with water. On treated pine it adds visible color, slows graying and repels water. Where it falls short of the premium oils is longevity on the deck floor. Owners commonly report that traffic paths and the sunniest boards need attention after one to two seasons, while railings last considerably longer. That is a reasonable trade if you want to spend less up front and are willing to recoat more often. Get the most out of it by cleaning and brightening the boards, letting them dry fully, applying two thin coats rather than one thick coat, and staining when the boards are out of direct sun. Buy all the gallons you need at once and have them boxed together, since store tinting can vary slightly between batches."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "PT Deck Stain Picks",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-deck-stain-for-pressure-treated-wood/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How soon can a new treated deck be stained?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on how wet the lumber was and your climate. Kiln-dried-after-treatment boards can often be stained within a few weeks. Standard wet treated pine commonly needs one to several months. The reliable check is the sprinkle test: if water soaks into the boards within about ten minutes, they are ready for a penetrating stain."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need to clean brand-new treated lumber before staining?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. New boards carry dirt, footprints, sawdust and sometimes a smooth, compressed surface from milling that limits how much stain the wood can absorb. Wash with an oxygen-based deck cleaner, rinse, apply a wood brightener and let the deck dry fully. That simple step helps the stain soak in evenly and last longer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will stain stick to treated wood that still looks green?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The green tint itself does not stop stain from bonding; moisture does. If the boards pass the sprinkle test, stain will penetrate even if a faint green color is still visible. Choose a warm cedar or brown tone to neutralize the tint, or a semi-solid stain if you want a cooler color."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is solid stain a bad idea on a treated deck?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not always, but it is riskier. Solid stain forms a film that can crack along the checks treated pine develops and peel on horizontal boards. It works best on older decks with mismatched or patched boards, on very dry wood, and on railings. For new treated decks, semi-transparent penetrating stains are the safer choice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does stain last on pressure-treated pine?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A semi-transparent penetrating stain typically lasts two to three years on a deck floor and longer on railings. Clear or translucent finishes may need yearly attention. Solid stains can last longer on paper but tend to peel when they fail. Sun, shade, foot traffic and prep quality all move those numbers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I use oil or water-based stain on treated wood?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Penetrating oil stains are the most forgiving on treated pine because they soak deep into the open grain and fade instead of peeling. Water-based stains work well if you need low odor or live in a strict VOC state, but they need cleaner wood and more careful application. Either works on a properly prepared deck."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I have to strip the deck before recoating?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not if you used a penetrating stain and it has worn evenly. Clean, brighten and apply one thin coat of the same product. Stripping is needed when the old finish is peeling, when you are switching between oil and water-based products, or when you want to move to a lighter, more transparent color."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Deck & Wood Finishes",
    tags: [
      "best deck stain for pressure treated wood",
      "pressure treated deck stain",
      "staining pt lumber",
      "stain for treated pine",
      "new deck staining",
      "semi-transparent stain"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The Best Stain for <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Pressure-Treated</span> Decks
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Treated pine arrives soaked, slightly green and hungry for oil once it dries. The right stain and the right timing turn it into a deck that looks finished for years.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ready-seal-natural-cedar"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Compare All Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="14.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Top 6 Picks</a>
          <a href="#which-pick" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Which Pick Fits</a>
          <a href="#why-different" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Why PT Is Different</a>
          <a href="#readiness" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. When It Is Ready</a>
          <a href="#stain-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Stain Types on PT</a>
          <a href="#green-tint" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Color on Treated Wood</a>
          <a href="#prep" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Prep Steps</a>
          <a href="#application" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Applying the Stain</a>
          <a href="#cut-ends" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Cut Ends & Hardware</a>
          <a href="#railings" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Railings & Stairs</a>
          <a href="#upkeep" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Cost & Upkeep</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">13. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Pressure-treated southern yellow pine is the lumber most American decks are built from, and it has two quirks that decide which stain works: it starts out wet from the treatment process, and it is porous enough to drink a penetrating stain once it dries. Wait for the wood, then choose a stain that soaks in rather than one that sits on top.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Stains for Pressure-Treated Wood
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these on manufacturer specifications (penetration, resin type, VOC compliance, recoat requirements), on what each label says about treated lumber, and on the patterns that show up repeatedly in owner feedback on treated-pine decks. We did not run our own exposure test, and no brand paid for a spot.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="which-pick" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Which Pick Fits Your Treated Deck
          </h2>

          <p>
            The six stains above overlap, so it helps to start from the situation rather than the brand. Most treated decks fall into one of a handful of scenarios, and each one points to a different product. Use the table as a shortcut, then read the full card for the stain that matches.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Your situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Start with</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">First deck, nervous about streaks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ready Seal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Built to avoid lap marks without back-brushing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shade, trees, recurring mildew</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">TWP 1500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mildewcides and deep penetration</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Long-term, low-effort upkeep</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Armstrong Clark</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flexible oils that fade evenly</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Need low odor or strict VOC state</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">DEFY Extreme</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water-based with mineral UV blockers</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Covered deck, want a rich tone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cabot Timber Oil</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Translucent oil that warms the grain</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tight budget, happy to recoat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Behr Semi-Transparent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cheap, tinted in store, easy to find</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If two scenarios apply, let sun exposure break the tie. A treated deck that gets six or more hours of direct sun needs the most pigment you are comfortable with, which favors semi-transparent or semi-solid versions over translucent oils. A deck that is shaded most of the day needs mildew resistance more than heavy pigment, which favors TWP or a water-based stain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think about the next five years, not just this weekend. A stain that is cheaper today but needs a floor recoat every year will cost more time than a premium penetrating oil that goes two or three years. If you value your Saturdays, the higher upfront price usually wins. If you rent, or plan to sell soon, the budget option is reasonable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, stick with one system once you have chosen it. Recoating with the same product, or at least the same chemistry, is what keeps maintenance simple. Switching brands every cycle invites bonding problems and color mismatches, and it can force a full strip that would otherwise never have been needed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-different" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why Treated Pine Needs Its Own Approach
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pressure-treated lumber is usually southern yellow pine that has been forced full of a waterborne preservative under pressure. Today that preservative is almost always copper-based, such as micronized copper azole (MCA) or alkaline copper quaternary (ACQ), which is why new boards often have a faint green or brown tint. The treatment protects against rot and insects. It does not protect against sun, water absorption or cracking, which is why the boards still need a finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            The treatment process leaves boards saturated. Lumber sold as wet treated wood can feel cool and heavy, and if you drive a screw into it you may see moisture. Stain cannot soak into wood that is already full of water, so anything you put on too early sits on the surface, then peels or wears away quickly as the wood dries beneath it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once it dries, treated pine is the opposite: open-grained and thirsty. It absorbs penetrating stain readily, which is good, but it also absorbs unevenly between the softer earlywood and denser latewood rings. That is why semi-transparent stains with real pigment look better on treated pine than clear finishes or very light toners, which can look blotchy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treated lumber also moves a lot. As it dries it shrinks, checks and sometimes cups or twists. A rigid, film-forming finish has to stretch across all of that movement, and it cracks along the checks. Penetrating stains avoid the problem because there is no surface film to break. Our overview of <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">deck stains for every type of deck</a> explains the wider choice between penetrating and film-forming products.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is one more wrinkle: treated lumber from the store is not consistent. Boards in the same stack can come from different mills, have different amounts of heartwood and dry at different rates. On a finished deck that shows up as boards that absorb stain differently, so one board may look darker or lighter than its neighbors. Cleaning and brightening narrows the gap, and a pigmented semi-transparent stain hides most of what is left.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="readiness" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Tell When New Treated Boards Are Ready
          </h2>

          <p>
            The old advice to wait six months before staining treated wood is a rough guide, not a rule. Drying time depends on how wet the boards were at the store, your climate, sun exposure and airflow under the deck. A deck in the dry, sunny Southwest can be ready in a few weeks. The same lumber under shade in a humid Gulf Coast summer can take several months.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SPRINKLE TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Water that soaks in means go. Water that beads means wait.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Flick a small amount of water onto several boards, including some in the shadiest part of the deck. If the drops absorb within about ten minutes, the wood is generally ready to accept a penetrating stain. If they bead or sit, give it another few weeks and test again.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            A pin-type moisture meter takes the guesswork out. Many stain manufacturers list a maximum moisture content on their labels, commonly somewhere around 12 to 15 percent. Readings vary across a deck, so take several on different boards and in shaded areas, and go by the highest reading rather than the average.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want to stain right away, look for <strong>KDAT</strong> lumber, which stands for kiln dried after treatment. These boards are dried in a kiln after they are treated, so they are ready for stain much sooner, often within a few weeks of installation once they have been cleaned. KDAT costs more and is not stocked everywhere, but it avoids months of waiting and reduces warping. If you are on the fence about whether your boards can take a finish at all, read <a href="/can-you-stain-pressure-treated-wood/">can you stain pressure-treated wood</a>.
          </p>

