        <div id="stain-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Stain Types Suit Treated Pine
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Oil-based penetrating semi-transparent stains</strong> are the most reliable choice on pressure-treated decks. They soak into the open grain, add enough pigment to block UV and wear by fading rather than peeling. Ready Seal, TWP and Armstrong Clark all fall into this group, and you will find more in our list of the <a href="/best-oil-based-deck-stain/">best oil-based deck stains</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Water-based semi-transparent stains</strong> are a solid second choice, especially in strict VOC states or if solvent odor is a problem. The better ones penetrate rather than film, but most need cleaner wood and more careful application than oils. They also tend to need recoating a bit sooner on the floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Solid stains</strong> can be used on treated pine and sometimes make sense on old, mismatched decks. But because they form a film, they are more likely to crack along checks and peel on horizontal boards. If you choose one, put it on only after the wood is thoroughly dry, and accept that you are committing to solid colors for the life of the deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clear water sealers</strong> help treated lumber shed water while it dries and can be a reasonable stopgap on a new deck during its first season. They give little UV protection, so the wood will still turn gray. See our <a href="/best-deck-sealant/">deck sealant</a> guide if that is your plan, and follow it with a pigmented stain once the boards have dried.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid any product that is not labeled for exterior horizontal wood. Interior wood stains, fence-only formulas and some siding products are not made to handle the abrasion and standing water that a deck floor sees. The label should say deck, and ideally it should list pressure-treated lumber among the approved surfaces. If the can does not mention decks at all, keep shopping.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="green-tint" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Choosing a Color for Green-Tinted Lumber
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fresh treated lumber often carries a green or olive cast from copper in the preservative. Translucent stains in light tones do not cover it well, and the green can read through as a muddy or khaki color. Warm mid-tones such as cedar, redwood, light brown and honey neutralize the green because their orange and red components cancel it out.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you prefer gray or weathered-wood tones, choose a semi-solid or solid version. A semi-transparent gray over green-tinted pine can look uneven or slightly greenish, especially in the first year as the treatment chemistry continues to fade. Test before you buy in quantity.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember that treated pine grows lighter and more gray as it weathers, and its color evens out after a season. A sample on a brand-new board will look different from the same stain applied after the deck has dried for a few months. Test on a board that has gone through the same drying and cleaning as the rest of the deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a fuller look at color families, opacity and how to match decking to siding and trim, see our <a href="/deck-stain-colors-guide/">guide to choosing deck stain colors</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more color consideration is heat. Dark solid stains absorb much more sunlight than light ones, and a dark deck floor in full summer sun can become uncomfortable for bare feet. Darker colors also show pollen, dust and scuffs. Mid-tones are the practical compromise: enough pigment for UV protection, light enough to stay cooler and hide everyday dirt. If your deck is mostly a play area for kids or pets, that matters more than the color chip.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Prep Steps for New and Weathered Treated Decks
          </h2>

          <p>
            New treated boards still need cleaning before stain, even if they look clean. Construction leaves dirt, footprints, sawdust and sometimes a slick surface from the milling process. Apply an oxygen-based deck cleaner, scrub with a stiff brush and rinse thoroughly. Follow with a wood brightener, which opens the grain and evens out the absorption.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weathered treated decks need more. Gray, oxidized fibers on the surface prevent stain from reaching sound wood, so they have to be cleaned off. A stronger deck cleaner, a stiff brush and a low-pressure rinse usually do the job. If there is an old coating, use a stripper made for it and then a brightener to neutralize it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pressure washing is helpful but easy to overdo. Treated pine is soft, and a narrow tip or high pressure will carve grooves and raise a fuzzy surface. Use a 25- or 40-degree tip, keep it moving with the grain and stay a foot or more from the boards. A model with adjustable pressure, like those in our <a href="/best-pressure-washer/">pressure washer</a> guide, gives you more control.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let the deck dry for at least two days after washing, longer in cool or humid weather, then repeat the sprinkle test. Sand any raised grain lightly with 60 to 80 grit and sweep off all the dust. Staining over a damp or dusty surface is one of the most common reasons treated decks look blotchy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect the surroundings before you open the stain. Cover shrubs and flower beds with breathable drop cloths, tape plastic over the bottom course of siding next to the deck, and lay cardboard over any patio or concrete below the deck edge where drips could land. Oil stain that soaks into concrete is very hard to remove, and stain on vinyl siding needs to be wiped off immediately with the solvent on the label. Ten minutes of masking saves an afternoon of cleanup.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Applying Stain to Pressure-Treated Boards
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choose a calm day with temperatures in the label range, usually about 50 to 90 degrees Fahrenheit, and no rain forecast for at least a day. Stain in the morning or evening when the boards are out of direct sun. Treated pine absorbs stain quickly on a hot day, and the surface flash-dries before the stain can soak in evenly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the railings, balusters and posts, then do the stairs, then the floor. On the floor, work along two or three boards at a time from one end to the other. Do not stop in the middle of a board. If you must break, stop at a joint or the end of a board.
          </p>

          <p>
            A pump sprayer or roller speeds things up, but follow with a brush to push the stain into the grain and catch any drips or puddles. Do not forget the edges and ends of boards, which absorb moisture and benefit most from protection. After the time listed on the label, wipe off any stain that is still sitting on the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Apply only as much as the wood can absorb. Some products call for a second coat wet-on-wet; others want one coat. A second coat applied after the first has dried rarely penetrates and often leads to a sticky or peeling surface. Our <a href="/deck-stain-drying-time/">deck stain drying time</a> guide explains how long to wait before walking on the deck and putting furniture back.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cut-ends" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Cut Ends, Fasteners and Ground Contact
          </h2>

          <p>
            The preservative in treated lumber does not always penetrate to the center of the board. When you cut a board during construction, you can expose untreated wood. Industry guidance from the American Wood Protection Association recommends brushing a field-treatment preservative, typically one containing copper naphthenate, onto cut ends, holes and notches. Do this before the board goes in place, especially on posts and joists that will stay damp.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stain is not a substitute for that end-cut treatment. It adds a water repellent and UV protection to the surface, but it is not formulated to carry enough preservative to protect exposed heartwood in ground contact or in constant moisture. Treat the cuts first, let them dry, and then stain the visible surfaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Copper-based treatments are corrosive to plain steel. Fasteners and connectors should be hot-dip galvanized or stainless steel, as specified by the lumber supplier and connector manufacturer. That has nothing to do with stain chemistry, but rusting fasteners will streak a fresh finish with dark stains, and corroded connectors are a structural problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep the deck floor clear of planters and rugs that trap water. Even the best stain will fail under a pot that keeps the boards wet for weeks, and the dark ring left behind is very hard to remove.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="railings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Railings, Stairs and Vertical Surfaces
          </h2>

          <p>
            Railings, balusters, posts and skirt boards take far less punishment than the floor. They do not carry foot traffic, they shed water instead of holding it, and they catch the sun at a steeper angle. As a result, the same stain that lasts two years on the floor commonly lasts four or more on the railings. That difference is worth planning around.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many owners use the same semi-transparent stain everywhere so the color matches, then recoat only the floor and stair treads on the short cycle. Others choose a semi-solid or solid stain for the railings and a semi-transparent stain on the floor, which gives the railings a crisp, uniform look while keeping the walking surface easy to maintain. If you go that route, test both colors side by side so the combination works.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stair treads deserve special attention. They take concentrated foot traffic, collect grit and often sit in the sun. Apply the stain carefully to the front edges and the nosings, which wear first, and check them every spring. A quick touch-up on the treads each year keeps the stairs from looking worn long before the rest of the deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            Balusters are tedious to stain with a brush. A pump sprayer with a drop cloth beneath, followed by a quick brush or mitt pass, is the fastest method. A staining mitt, which is a glove with a thick nap, lets you wrap your hand around round or square spindles and coat all four sides in one pass.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Cost, Coverage and Long-Term Upkeep
          </h2>

          <p>
            Treated pine is absorbent, so plan on the lower end of the coverage range on the label, especially for the first coat. For a typical 12 by 16 foot treated deck with railings, that usually means 3 to 4 gallons of a penetrating oil stain, including a wet-on-wet second coat where the label calls for one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rough Budget for a 200 Sq Ft Treated Deck</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
