            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those figures are approximate ranges based on typical gallon prices and label coverage. Premium stains cost more at the start but usually need fewer and simpler recoats, so the cost per year tends to be lower. Budget water-based stains cost less now but often mean a recoat every one or two years on the floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Each spring, sweep and wash the deck with a mild cleaner and check the high-traffic paths. When water stops beading and the color looks faded, clean, brighten and apply one thin maintenance coat of the same stain. Keep a quart from the original batch for touch-ups. If you are also finishing a treated fence, our <a href="/best-fence-stain/">fence stain</a> guide covers products made for large vertical areas.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining the Week the Deck Is Built</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wet treated lumber cannot absorb stain, so the coating sits on top and fails as the boards dry. Unless the lumber is kiln dried after treatment, run the sprinkle test or use a moisture meter and wait until the boards actually pass, even if that means staining next season.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Clear Finish as the Final Coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clear water sealers keep water out but do almost nothing against UV light, so treated pine turns gray anyway, often unevenly. Use a clear sealer only as a first-season stopgap if you must, then switch to a pigmented semi-transparent stain once the boards have dried properly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blasting the Boards With a Narrow Tip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Treated pine is soft. A 0- or 15-degree tip at close range gouges the surface and raises fibers that soak up stain unevenly and splinter later. Use a wide fan tip on low pressure, keep it moving with the grain, and let a cleaner and brush do most of the work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting to Treat Cut Ends</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cutting treated lumber can expose wood the preservative never reached. Stain alone does not protect it. Brush a copper naphthenate end-cut preservative onto cuts, notches and drilled holes during construction, especially on posts, joists and anything that will stay damp, before any stain goes on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Picking a Light Gray Over Green Wood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A light semi-transparent gray over fresh, copper-tinted pine often reads as a muddy green. Warm cedar and brown tones cancel the green out. If you want gray, choose a semi-solid or solid gray and test it on a cleaned, dry board first before buying gallons.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How soon can a new treated deck be stained?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on how wet the lumber was and your climate. Kiln-dried-after-treatment boards can often be stained within a few weeks. Standard wet treated pine commonly needs one to several months. The reliable check is the sprinkle test: if water soaks into the boards within about ten minutes, they are ready for a penetrating stain.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to clean brand-new treated lumber before staining?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. New boards carry dirt, footprints, sawdust and sometimes a smooth, compressed surface from milling that limits how much stain the wood can absorb. Wash with an oxygen-based deck cleaner, rinse, apply a wood brightener and let the deck dry fully. That simple step helps the stain soak in evenly and last longer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will stain stick to treated wood that still looks green?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The green tint itself does not stop stain from bonding; moisture does. If the boards pass the sprinkle test, stain will penetrate even if a faint green color is still visible. Choose a warm cedar or brown tone to neutralize the tint, or a semi-solid stain if you want a cooler color.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is solid stain a bad idea on a treated deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not always, but it is riskier. Solid stain forms a film that can crack along the checks treated pine develops and peel on horizontal boards. It works best on older decks with mismatched or patched boards, on very dry wood, and on railings. For new treated decks, semi-transparent penetrating stains are the safer choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does stain last on pressure-treated pine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A semi-transparent penetrating stain typically lasts two to three years on a deck floor and longer on railings. Clear or translucent finishes may need yearly attention. Solid stains can last longer on paper but tend to peel when they fail. Sun, shade, foot traffic and prep quality all move those numbers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use oil or water-based stain on treated wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Penetrating oil stains are the most forgiving on treated pine because they soak deep into the open grain and fade instead of peeling. Water-based stains work well if you need low odor or live in a strict VOC state, but they need cleaner wood and more careful application. Either works on a properly prepared deck.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I have to strip the deck before recoating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not if you used a penetrating stain and it has worn evenly. Clean, brighten and apply one thin coat of the same product. Stripping is needed when the old finish is peeling, when you are switching between oil and water-based products, or when you want to move to a lighter, more transparent color.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

